      "prop:audit-table-equivalence"
    ],
    "justification": "The iff theorem proves that the finite incidence rule is both sufficient and necessary for the causal vector, rather than merely another recovery recipe.",
    "gap": "No cited result supplies a complete partial-audit criterion for this arbitrary-horizon single-hazard Markov family.",
    "consumer": "The theorem decides whether a planned partial recontact supports every treatment-specific endpoint probability under the declared graph.",
    "proof_tex": "The first equivalence is \\(\\text{def:criterion}\\), whose right-hand side is the finite incidence test \\(\\mathcal H(B)\\ne\\varnothing\\) and does not invoke identification.  Suppose first that this set is nonempty.  By \\(\\text{thm:single-hazard-alternative}\\), every compatible law satisfies\n\\[\nQ(P)=\\mathsf{Rec}^{(1)}_{T,k,j,B}((M_d(P))_{d=0}^T).\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Phi_{T,S,B,e}(P)=\\Phi_{T,S,B,e}(P')\\), then \\(\\text{prop:audit-table-equivalence}\\) gives \\(M_d(P)=M_d(P')\\) for every \\(d\\).  Applying the same deterministic map in (23) yields\n\\[\nQ(P)=\\mathsf{Rec}^{(1)}_{T,k,j,B}((M_d(P))_d)\n=\\mathsf{Rec}^{(1)}_{T,k,j,B}((M_d(P'))_d)=Q(P').\n\\]\nThis is precisely \\(\\operatorname{GID}(T,S,B)=1\\) by \\(\\text{def:graphwise-identification}\\).\n\nConversely, if \\(\\mathcal H(B)=\\varnothing\\), the obstruction branch of \\(\\text{thm:single-hazard-alternative}\\) supplies \\(P,P'\\in\\mathcal P_{T,S,B,e}\\) having equal observed laws and unequal endpoint vectors.  This pair violates the implication in \\(\\text{def:graphwise-identification}\\), so \\(\\operatorname{GID}(T,S,B)=0\\).  This proves the second equivalence and identifies the outputs of both branches.  The recovery proof used only positive scalar normalization and Markov factorization, not transition-matrix inversion, so deficient ranks and null allowed-arrow effects are included.",
